<!-- The use cases — full pages at scale, rather than a case or a rung.

     Everything else this site shows is small on purpose: a rung is one file introducing one thing, a
     case is one body making one claim, a bench is one arm against a hand-written equivalent. These are
     the other end — a thousand keyed rows, a filtered table, a payload five levels deep over an rpc —
     and what they demonstrate is the shape an APP has rather than the shape a primitive has.

     They used to be a separate application, framed here, reachable only if `bun run fleet` happened to
     be running. The separation bought a shell with no stylesheet, and nothing shown here needed one:
     what `/demos/<name>` reports is DOM CALLS, which are exact and which no stylesheet moves. The
     pricing claim that DID need an unstyled arm is the cross-repo comparison in ~/code, where the
     harness installs the same rules into every framework's arm.

     An INDEX rather than six demos on one page, and that is a cost decision: `complex` alone builds a
     thousand keyed rows and `data` fetches about ten thousand objects, so a page holding all six would
     drive twelve ops against six live demos before a reader had chosen one. -->
<script module>
import { USECASES } from '#shared/demos/usecases/USECASES.ts'
</script>

<div class="page-head">
    <h1 class="page-title">demos</h1>
    <p class="page-blurb">Full use cases, each an ordinary component this app renders inline. Open one to watch it run, read the files it is written in, and see what its ops cost in DOM calls.</p>
</div>

<div class="cards">
    {#for usecase of USECASES by usecase.name}
        <a class="card" href="/demos/{usecase.name}">
            <p class="card-tag">{usecase.ops.length === 0 ? 'reports its own numbers' : `${usecase.ops.length} ops priced`}</p>
            <h2 class="card-title">{usecase.name}</h2>
            <p class="card-blurb">{usecase.blurb}</p>
        </a>
    {/for}
</div>

<p class="note">What is measured on a demo page is what its ops DID — DOM calls, and a coarse wall clock. What one cost the ENGINE is <code>bun run e2e</code>’s to say: style, layout and paint are only reachable over the devtools protocol, which a page cannot speak. The demo proves it works; the engine run prices it.</p>
